## Patterns that repeat across different accounts
1. The AI's calm, polite reply to human chaos (toxic breakup with ChatGPT, rage-bait yelling, "Good catch"). Comments paste their own AI's reply.
2. Dependency: usage limit / Claude down / day without Claude at the worst moment.
3. AI does the work, the human takes the credit (boss, interview, essay).
4. AI fails something trivial, or redoes one tiny thing forever (bad prompts, sixty-one times, seems simple enough).
5. Claude as the one with principles (sacrifice test, RESPECT); AI rivalry (Claude vs ChatGPT/Codex/Gemini).
6. Fear/fun of the AI remembering everything (manners, rants, "throws a party when you leave").
7. Money: the token/API bill.
8. Line 1 states the problem; the last 3 seconds hold a screenshot-able punchline; caption asks a question people answer in comments.


PRODUCTION (Blender, what we can actually show): Cleo at her desk (look at Claude/monitor/phone, hands to face, lean, type, slump, smile, brow); Claude on the desk (face her/monitor/phone, nod, shake, tilt, hop, puff up, glow). Shots: wide, two-shot, Cleo medium/close-up, Claude close-up, monitor insert, phone insert, whiteboard insert. Monitor/phone inserts can show: terminal/agent log, a web page or document, an email, a phone notification, a chat thread with typing dots, code, a whiteboard with handwriting. Other people exist only as texts, emails, notifications or off-screen voices. Night or day room.

HARD RULES (a script that breaks any rule is rejected):
R1. Line 1 is spoken by Cleo, at most 8 words, starts with "Claude." and states a shocking stake or a strange situation. No setup, no greeting, no text in the first frame.
R2. By second 3 the viewer must know what is at stake. Every line must raise stakes, add a reveal, or land a joke. No filler, no explaining.
R3. Must be understood by anyone who has used ChatGPT/Claude, not only programmers. Code can appear, but the joke must not depend on knowing code.
R4. A twist in the last 25% that recontextualizes everything before it (like "I copied it"). The twist must be set up earlier in plain sight.
R5. A final line or final visual that people would screenshot, quote in comments, or send to a friend ("this is literally me"). Built-in comment bait: a question the viewer wants to answer or an experience they want to confess.
R6. Claude stays in character: literal, calm, loyal, deadpan. Warm is allowed. No lesson, no moral, no sad ending.
R7. NO romance, dating, exes, crushes or relationships between humans (our last two videos were about an ex; the owner wants a different scope). Do NOT reuse these stories either: texting an ex / report to an ex, deleted database + backup, Claude watching reels, redo 61 times, API key leak, test email to 40k customers, useEffect loop, CORS, git bisect, power cut/battery, center a div, password reset TODO, force push merge conflict, AI bill from "think harder".
R8. One location, filmable with the limits above.

R9. The three stories must each hit a DIFFERENT pattern from the research list below (no two on the same theme).

DELIVERABLE: exactly 3 complete stories. For each:
- TITLE
- HOOK (line 1)
- WHY IT SPREADS: the single human feeling it hits, and who will tag whom (one line each)
- FULL SCRIPT: numbered lines "CLEO: ..." / "CLAUDE: ...", plus [bracketed one-line visual notes] only where a screen/phone/visual beat matters.
- FINAL FRAME: what is on screen in the last second.
- CAPTION IDEA (one line) and the COMMENT BAIT question.
- SELF-SCORE 1-10 on: hook, retention, twist, shareability. Be harsh. If any score is below 8, rewrite the story before answering until it is 8+.
Then: which ONE of your three you would bet the channel on, and why, in two lines.
Write in plain text. No preamble.

